  "text_md": "LORD-FEST. Excessively lordly. (A.-S.)\n.\nLORDINGS. Sirs ; masters. (A.~S.) It is often\n1567.\nxviii, 313-335\nStede and armure alle was blake.\nLORDS-AND-LADIES. See Bulls-and-Cows. LORDSHIP. Supreme power. (J.-S.)\nWhan thy mouthe with shryfte ys opun\nan unimportant personage in the olden times.\n- (4) The faggot wood of a tree. LOPE. Leapt. Also, to leap. It seems to be\nmons. North.\nLOOVEYNG. Praise ; honour.\n# LOP 529 LOR\nLOPPING. Lame. Dorset.\n- (2) To lollop or lounge about. Kent.\nOf cidre, palmS; and of torere.\nLOOVEYD. Praised. Ritson.\nHere lorefadyr ys thedevyl. MS, Harl. 1701, f 24. LOREINE. A rein. See Launfal, 888.\nLOPUSTER. A lobster.\nAs a lopwebbe filetli fome and gnattis. Taken and suffren gret files go.\nLOPLOLLY. A lazy fellow. West.\nWas tumid, whiche is ever grene.\ntioned in Harrison's England, p. 230.\nMS.Harl.m^, f\nLOOT. A thin oblong square board fixed to a staff or handle, used in boiling brine to remove the scum. Staff.\nLORD-OF-MISRULE. The person who pre-\n- LORE. (1) Knowledge ; doctrine\nLOP. (1) A flea. North. {A.-S.) Ys joy y-now so ye your lyggys streyne, Ye lade longe-sydyde as a loppe.\nHampole, MS. Bowes, p. 25.\nHe began his rule on All-hallow eve and con-\nWhare he had no fode, Bot wlatsome glette and lopyrd blode.\nLOREMAR. A bit-maker. Palsgrave. \"Lori-\nGreat. Moral, p. 243.\nOf al men they do most evy].\nGower, MS. Soc. Antiq. 134, f. 96.\nCursor Mundi, MS. Coll. Trin. Cantab, f. 111. Sythen he ys lopen on hys stedc. He with hym Harrawde dud lede.\nLopen undir oure lordes wynge.\nLorels den, Holinshed, Chron. Ireland, p. 93.\nMS. Lincoln A. i. 17, f. 270. Thare dwelled a man in a myrke donjowne. And in a fowlestede of corrupcyowne,\nLOOVER. An opening at the top of a dove-cote.\nLOPIRD. Coagulated. Still iu use. See Forby,\nCocke Lorel was formerly a generic title for a very great rascal. \" Lasy lorrels,\" Harman,\nMS. Cantab. Ff. ii. 38, f. 154.\n(4) The privilege of turning out cattle on com-\nLORD-SIZE. The judge at the assizes. LORD'S-ROOM. The stage-box in a theatre\nabound. Var. dial.\nMS. Hail. 1701. f. 70. Theportar set the yatys open.\npences, dated in 1552, in Kempe's Loseley\n40.\na subst. in the second example. As sone as the chylde had spoke. The fende ynto hym was lope.\nDeth and synne are bothe oute lopun.\n![](<11_pg57_pg1_images/imageFile2.png>)\n(2) Lost. Still in use in Somerset.\nMS. Cantab. Ff. v. 48, f. 61.",
